Skip resolving trace-event enum maps, which single-stepping cannot afford.

eval_map_work_func() rewrites every trace event's print-format string,
replacing enum names with their numeric values so a tracefs reader sees
numbers. It does this by scanning each of ~1700 events' format strings against
each of ~1400 eval maps, in trace_event_update_all -> update_event_printk: on
the order of a million string comparisons, each re-walking a format string.

On real hardware that is a blip. A cooperative guest is single-stepped, one
world switch per batch, and at that scale the scan is a wall the boot cannot
get past -- it presents as a hang, hundreds of millions of instructions deep in
trace_event_update_all's inner character loop with no new console output and
the event/map iterators (verified from the guest registers: map index 1345 of
1414, scan pointer in .rodata) grinding forward far too slowly to ever finish.

The data is not corrupt -- the section bounds are right and the strings are
real -- it is simply O(events x maps x length) where none of the three is
small. And it is cosmetic: it changes how trace formats print in tracefs,
which this guest does not use and does not reach.

So skip it when co_colinux_guest. Guarded in eval_map_work_func() rather than
at the queue_work() site, because there is a fallback path that calls the
function directly when the workqueue cannot be allocated, and both want the
same treatment.

--- a/kernel/trace/trace.c
+++ b/kernel/trace/trace.c
@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@
 #include <linux/ring_buffer.h>
 #include <linux/utsname.h>
 #include <linux/stacktrace.h>
+#include <linux/console.h>	/* co_colinux_guest */
 #include <linux/writeback.h>
 #include <linux/kallsyms.h>
 #include <linux/security.h>
@@ -9050,6 +9051,23 @@
 {
 	int len;
 
+	/*
+	 * This resolves enum names to their values inside every trace event's
+	 * print-format string, so that a reader of tracefs sees numbers rather
+	 * than symbols. It does so by scanning each of ~1700 events' format
+	 * strings against each of ~1400 eval maps -- a million-plus string
+	 * comparisons, cosmetic and needed by nothing that boots.
+	 *
+	 * A cooperative guest is single-stepped, and at that scale the scan is
+	 * a wall a run cannot get past in any reasonable time -- it looks like
+	 * a hang, in trace_event_update_all, hundreds of millions of
+	 * instructions with no forward progress. Skipping it costs only the
+	 * enum-name resolution in tracefs output, which this guest has no user
+	 * for, and lets the boot continue to the next initcall.
+	 */
+	if (co_colinux_guest)
+		return;
+
 	len = __stop_ftrace_eval_maps - __start_ftrace_eval_maps;
 	trace_event_update_with_eval_map(NULL, __start_ftrace_eval_maps, len);
 }
